/// Chapter 16 of the guide, written from md-docs/16-content.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Content(path: "/docs/guide/content", title: "Content", description: "The Markdown element, .md pages with front matter, static builds, search and sharing tags, code and media.", layout: DocsShell("Content", chapter: "Chapter 16", blurb: "Markdown, .md pages, static builds, and what the build does for search engines and link previews.", sections: [Anchor(id: "the-markdown-element", label: "The Markdown element"), Anchor(id: "md-pages", label: ".md pages"), Anchor(id: "static-builds", label: "Static builds"), Anchor(id: "search-and-sharing", label: "Search and sharing"), Anchor(id: "code-on-the-page", label: "Code on the page"), Anchor(id: "images-and-media", label: "Images and media")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/i18n", prevLabel: "15. Internationalisation", nextTo: "/docs/guide/outputs", nextLabel: "17. Outputs")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("A site is also its words: pages of prose, a blog, docs. This chapter covers writing content in Markdown, pre-rendering the site to static HTML, and what the build does for search engines and link previews.")

        Heading("The Markdown element", id: "the-markdown-element").h2

        Markdown("`Markdown(text)` renders a small, predictable Markdown — the same at build time and in the browser, so a hydrated page repaints what was painted:")

        CodeBlock("page Notes(path: \"/\") \{\n    state body = \"# Release notes\\n\\nVersion **3.3** adds:\\n\\n- Markdown pages\\n- `wf docs`\\n\\n> Ship it.\"\n    Markdown(body)\n    Markdown(\"Inline *emphasis*, a [link](/about) and an ![image](/img.png).\")\n\}", title: "wf")

        Markdown("Blocks: `#`–`######` headings, paragraphs, fenced code with a language (`` ```js ``), `>` quotes, `-`/`*` and `1.` lists (one level), `---` rules. Inline: `` `code` ``, `**strong**`, `*em*`/`_em_`, links, images. Raw HTML in the text is shown as text, not run — Markdown from a user or an API is safe to render.")

        Heading(".md pages", id: "md-pages").h2

        Markdown("A Markdown file in `src/` is a page. Its front matter names the page's attributes; the rest is the body:")

        CodeBlock("---\npath: /guide/install\ntitle: Installing\ndescription: Get wf on your machine in a minute.\nlayout: DocsShell\ntype: article\n---\n\n# Installing\n\nDownload a release, or build from source:\n\n```sh\ncargo install webfluent\n```\n\nThen `wf init my-site`.", title: "markdown")

        Markdown("The front matter keys are the page's attributes: `path` (default: `/` for `index.md`, else `/<file-name>`), `title`, `description`, `layout`, `image`, `type`, `noindex`. The page's name is the file's stem, capitalised.\n\n`layout: DocsShell` frames the body in a component with a default slot — the same one a `.wf` page names ([chapter 3](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing#layouts)):")

        CodeBlock("component DocsShell \{\n    slot\n    Row(gap: .lg) \{\n        Sidebar \{\n            Sidebar.Item(to: \"/guide/install\") \{ Text(\"Installing\") \}\n            Sidebar.Item(to: \"/guide/pages\") \{ Text(\"Pages\") \}\n        \}\n        Container \{ children \}\n    \}\n\}", title: "wf")

        Markdown("A docs site is a folder of `.md` files, one layout, and a `.wf` for the `app`. (`wf docs` is something else: a gallery of every component the project can use — [chapter 18](/docs/guide/tooling#wf-docs).)")

        Heading("Static builds", id: "static-builds").h2

        Markdown("With `\"build\": \{ \"ssg\": true \}` every page is pre-rendered to HTML at build time: `index.html`, `about/index.html`, `posts/hello/index.html`, one per route (and one per value in `paths:` for a `:param` page). Each file is the page's first paint — text, lists over seeded state, components expanded, the `loading` arm of every resource — with the app script loading after it. The page is readable before any script runs, and interactive once the script hydrates the paint in place, without repainting it.\n\nWhat the static paint knows: literals, `state` initial values, `derived` over them, `data`, `const`, `env`, `t` in the default locale, `format` and `ago` with a built-in locale table. What it does not: fetched data, the browser's values (`viewport`, `query`, `hash`, `theme`), anything read from `window`. A condition on an unknown renders its live branch once hydrated.\n\nThe output also holds:")

        Table(caption: "Static builds") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("File").header
                    Table.Cell("").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`404.html`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The `path: \"*\"` page, served by a static host for a route it has no file for") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sitemap.xml`, `robots.txt`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every indexable route (`meta.sitemap`, on by default; needs `meta.site_url`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`styles.css`, `pages/*.css`") }
                    Table.Cell("Shared and per-page stylesheets")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`app.js`, `pages/*.js`") }
                    Table.Cell("The runtime and each page's script, loaded when its route shows")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`*.gz`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Each text file, precompressed (`build.compress`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`_headers`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A strict Content-Security-Policy for hosts that read one (`build.csp`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`public/*`") }
                    Table.Cell("Copied as they are to the output root")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Search and sharing", id: "search-and-sharing").h2

        Markdown("For every page the build writes the standard head from the page's attributes and `meta.*` in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock("\{\n  \"meta\": \{\n    \"title\": \"Acme\",\n    \"description\": \"Tools for small teams.\",\n    \"lang\": \"en\",\n    \"site_url\": \"https://acme.example\",\n    \"site_name\": \"Acme Inc.\",\n    \"image\": \"/og-default.png\",\n    \"favicon\": \"/favicon.svg\",\n    \"sitemap\": true,\n    \"fonts\": [\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter&display=swap\"],\n    \"stylesheets\": [\"/print.css\"]\n  \}\n\}", title: "json")

        Markdown("- `<title>` and `<meta name=\"description\">` from the page's `title:` and `description:` — the snippet a search result shows and a link preview quotes. Keep the description under ~150 characters.\n- A canonical link and `og:url`, when `site_url` is set (a relative canonical is worse than none, so without `site_url` the build emits none).\n- Open Graph and Twitter card tags: `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:image` (the page's `image:` or the site's), `og:type` from `type:` (`website` or `article`), `og:site_name`.\n- JSON-LD: an `Organization`, a `WebPage` or `Article`, and a `BreadcrumbList` derived from the route's segments.\n- `hreflang` alternates for each locale when the project has several.\n- `<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex\">` and no sitemap entry for a `noindex: true` page.\n\nA page adds its own tags with `head \{ \}` ([chapter 3](/docs/guide/pages-and-routing#per-page-head-tags)).")

        CodeBlock("type Post \{ slug: String, title: String, summary: String, cover: String, date: String, body: String \}\n\ndata posts: [Post] = \"posts.json\"\n\npage PostPage(path: \"/posts/:slug\", slug: String, type: \"article\", paths: posts.map(p => p.slug)) \{\n    derived post = posts.find(p => p.slug == slug)\n    head \{\n        meta(property: \"og:title\", content: post?.title ?? \"Post\")\n        meta(property: \"og:image\", content: post?.cover ?? \"/og-default.png\")\n        meta(property: \"article:published_time\", content: post?.date ?? \"\")\n    \}\n    if let p = post \{\n        Image(src: p.cover, alt: p.title)\n        Heading(p.title).h1\n        Text(format(p.date, .date, \"long\")).muted\n        Markdown(p.body)\n    \}\n\}", title: "wf")

        Markdown("`title:` and `description:` of a `:param` page are one string for every value, so name the section there (\"Blog\") and let the `og:title` in `head \{ \}` carry each post's own; the runtime keeps the head tags current as the parameter changes.")

        Heading("Code on the page", id: "code-on-the-page").h2

        Markdown("`Code(\"…\")` is inline code; `Code(\"…\").block` a block. `language:` colours it — `wf`, `json`, `bash` or `css` — at build time and in the browser alike, with the theme's `syntax-*` tokens; a `Markdown` fence with one of those languages is coloured the same way.")

        CodeBlock("page Snippets(path: \"/\") \{\n    Text(\"Run \")  Code(\"wf serve\")  Text(\" to start.\")\n    Code(\"page Home(path: \\\"/\\\") \{\\n    Text(\\\"Hi\\\")\\n\}\", language: \"wf\").block\n    Code(\"$ wf build\", language: \"bash\").block\n\}", title: "wf")

        Heading("Images and media", id: "images-and-media").h2

        CodeBlock("page Media(path: \"/\") \{\n    Image(src: \"/hero.jpg\", alt: \"The team at the launch\", width: 1200, height: 630)\n    Image(src: \"/deco.png\", alt: \"\")\n    Video(src: \"/demo.mp4\", controls: true)\n\}", title: "wf")

        Markdown("Give every `Image` an `alt:` — empty for a decorative one — and a `width:` and `height:` where you know them so the layout does not shift; the first image on a page loads eagerly and later ones lazily. Files in `public/` are served from the root.")
    }
}
